window.SCEN = [{"title":"A process can't write its logs any more","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An application on a Linux server has stopped writing to its log file. It's still running.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What do you check first?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Reinstall the application","ok":false,"why":"<p>Too drastic before you know the cause.</p>"},{"t":"Disk space and inodes (<code>df -h</code>, <code>df -i</code>), then permissions and ownership on the log file and folder, then the logging config and service (rsyslog/journald)","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Full disks and permissions are the usual suspects.</p>"},{"t":"Increase the server's RAM","ok":false,"why":"<p>Writing logs needs disk space, not RAM.</p>"},{"t":"Reboot immediately","ok":false,"why":"<p>You'd lose evidence, and it may not fix a full disk.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> for \"can't write\" problems, check space, inodes and permissions first.</p>","topic":"Linux","level":"Junior","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s0-a-process-can-t-write-its-logs"},{"title":"Is this Linux server healthy?","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You've just been given SSH access to a server and asked \"is it healthy?\"</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which set of checks gives the best quick picture?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>uptime</code> (load), <code>free -h</code>, <code>df -h</code>/<code>df -i</code>, <code>systemctl --failed</code>, <code>journalctl -p err -b</code>, and <code>ss -tlnp</code> for expected listeners","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. CPU, memory, disk, services, errors and network in under a minute.</p>"},{"t":"<code>ls -la /</code>","ok":false,"why":"<p>That just lists files.</p>"},{"t":"<code>ping google.com</code>","ok":false,"why":"<p>It only tests outbound connectivity.</p>"},{"t":"<code>history</code>","ok":false,"why":"<p>It shows past commands, not health.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> have a checklist ready. The golden signals apply to servers too.</p>","topic":"Linux","level":"Junior","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s1-is-this-linux-server-healthy-"},{"title":"I can't reach a website","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A user says \"the website is down\". You can SSH to a machine on the same network.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the best troubleshooting order?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Restart the web server, then check","ok":false,"why":"<p>Changes before diagnosis destroy evidence and may not help.</p>"},{"t":"<code>dig</code> the name → <code>ping</code>/<code>traceroute</code> the IP → <code>nc -zv host 443</code> → <code>curl -v https://…</code> → server logs and listeners (<code>ss -tlnp</code>)","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. DNS, then routing, then the port, then the application. One layer at a time.</p>"},{"t":"Clear the browser cache and give up if that fails","ok":false,"why":"<p>Too shallow.</p>"},{"t":"Check the database first","ok":false,"why":"<p>Start at the edge and work in.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> a structured, layered approach. Say it out loud in interviews.</p>","topic":"Networking","level":"Mid","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s2-i-can-t-reach-a-website"},{"title":"Slow transfers between two hosts","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Copying data between two servers in the same data centre is extremely slow.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which approach is best?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Measure with <code>iperf3</code>, check per-hop latency and loss with <code>mtr</code>, check the interfaces for errors and duplex/MTU mismatches, and check whether the disk or CPU is the real bottleneck","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Measure first: the network may not even be the problem.</p>"},{"t":"Switch from TCP to UDP","ok":false,"why":"<p>Not without understanding the cause, and UDP gives up reliability.</p>"},{"t":"Reboot both servers","ok":false,"why":"<p>It may hide the symptom without fixing anything.</p>"},{"t":"Buy faster disks","ok":false,"why":"<p>Maybe, but only once measurements say so.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> isolate network vs disk vs CPU before fixing anything.</p>","topic":"Networking","level":"Mid","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s3-slow-transfers-between-two-hos"},{"title":"Resources in a private subnet need internet","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>App servers in private subnets must download updates from the internet, but must <strong>not</strong> be reachable from it.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What do you add?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Public IPs on the app servers","ok":false,"why":"<p>That makes them reachable from the internet.</p>"},{"t":"A NAT gateway in a public subnet, and a route <code>0.0.0.0/0 → NAT</code> in the private route table","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Outbound only, and replies come back. One NAT per AZ for resilience.</p>"},{"t":"An internet gateway route in the private route table","ok":false,"why":"<p>That makes the subnet public.</p>"},{"t":"A VPC peering connection","ok":false,"why":"<p>Peering connects VPCs, not the internet.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> IGW = two-way for public resources; NAT = outbound only for private ones.</p>","topic":"AWS","level":"Junior","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s4-resources-in-a-private-subnet-"},{"title":"Build a three-tier app on AWS","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>\"Design a classic three-tier web application on AWS.\"</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which design is best?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"One big EC2 instance running the web server, app and database","ok":false,"why":"<p>A single point of failure, and the database is exposed.</p>"},{"t":"A VPC across 2+ AZs: ALB (with ACM) in public subnets → app on an ASG or ECS in private subnets (from the ALB SG only) → RDS Multi-AZ in isolated subnets (from the app SG only), plus Route 53, Secrets Manager, CloudWatch and Terraform","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Each tier is isolated and redundant, and only reachable from the tier in front of it.</p>"},{"t":"Everything in public subnets with strong passwords","ok":false,"why":"<p>Passwords don't replace network isolation.</p>"},{"t":"Three Regions, one tier each","ok":false,"why":"<p>That adds latency and cost without isolating anything properly.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> draw it. Memos is this design minus the data tier.</p>","topic":"AWS","level":"Mid","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s5-build-a-three-tier-app-on-aws"},{"title":"The pipeline fails at the test stage","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>After your change, the CI pipeline fails in the automated test stage.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the best approach?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Re-run until it passes","ok":false,"why":"<p>That might hide a real regression, or a flaky test you should fix.</p>"},{"t":"Disable the failing test to unblock the merge","ok":false,"why":"<p>That defeats the purpose of CI.</p>"},{"t":"Read the logs to find which tests failed and why, decide if it's a real regression, a flaky test or the environment, reproduce it locally, then fix and re-run","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. The pipeline did its job by stopping bad code early.</p>"},{"t":"Merge anyway and fix later","ok":false,"why":"<p>Now everyone's builds are red.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> a red pipeline is a safety net, not an obstacle.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","level":"Junior","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s6-the-pipeline-fails-at-the-test"},{"title":"main keeps breaking","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Many developers merge frequently and <code>main</code> is broken several times a week.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What helps most?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Branch protection with required checks and reviews, small PRs, fast CI feedback, merge queues, and feature flags for unfinished work","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Make it impossible to merge red, and keep each change small.</p>"},{"t":"Only allow merges on Mondays","ok":false,"why":"<p>Bigger batches mean more breakage.</p>"},{"t":"One person reviews and merges everything","ok":false,"why":"<p>A bottleneck that doesn't scale.</p>"},{"t":"Turn off tests to go faster","ok":false,"why":"<p>Faster breakage.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> guardrails beat gatekeepers.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","level":"Mid","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s7-main-keeps-breaking"},{"title":"Green pipeline, broken staging","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>CI passes, but the app fails once deployed to staging.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What are the most likely causes?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The tests were too thorough","ok":false,"why":"<p>Thorough tests aren't the problem.</p>"},{"t":"Environment differences the tests didn't cover: config or env vars, missing secrets, IAM permissions, network rules, database schema or migrations, dependency versions","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Then add smoke and integration tests against staging, and define environments with IaC.</p>"},{"t":"Git history is corrupted","ok":false,"why":"<p>Very unlikely.</p>"},{"t":"The runner was too slow","ok":false,"why":"<p>Speed doesn't change behaviour like this.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> \"works in CI\" ≠ \"works in the environment\". Close the gap with IaC and smoke tests.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","level":"Mid","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s8-green-pipeline-broken-staging"},{"title":"Walk me through commit to production","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>\"Walk me through a CI/CD pipeline from code commit to production.\"</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which answer is strongest?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"PR opens → CI builds, lints, unit-tests and scans → one versioned artefact (image:sha) → deploy to staging → integration/smoke tests → approval (if continuous delivery) → same artefact to prod with a rolling or blue/green deployment → monitor and roll back automatically on failure","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Build once, promote everywhere, and close the loop with monitoring.</p>"},{"t":"Developer builds locally and copies files to the server","ok":false,"why":"<p>Manual, unrepeatable and risky.</p>"},{"t":"Deploy straight to production on every commit with no tests","ok":false,"why":"<p>Speed without safety.</p>"},{"t":"Build a different artefact for each environment","ok":false,"why":"<p>You'd ship something you never tested.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> then tell them how Memos does it: build.yml → deploy.yml → /healthz.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","level":"Mid","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s9-walk-me-through-commit-to-prod"},{"title":"Kubernetes pod in CrashLoopBackOff","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A deployment's pods are in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What do you do first?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Delete the node","ok":false,"why":"<p>The node is probably fine.</p>"},{"t":"<code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> and <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code> to see why the container exits (config, missing secret, OOM, failing probe)","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. The container keeps starting and dying, so read the last crash's logs and the events.</p>"},{"t":"Scale to 0 and forget it","ok":false,"why":"<p>That hides the problem.</p>"},{"t":"Restart the API server","ok":false,"why":"<p>The control plane isn't the issue.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> describe + logs --previous solves most pod problems.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","level":"Mid","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s10-kubernetes-pod-in-crashloopbac"},{"title":"A master node fails","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In a cluster with a single control-plane node, that node dies.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the impact, and how do you prevent it?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Every pod stops immediately","ok":false,"why":"<p>Running pods keep running on the workers.</p>"},{"t":"Running workloads continue, but nothing can be changed or rescheduled until it's back. Prevent it with 3+ control-plane nodes (etcd quorum) or a managed control plane such as EKS","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. That's control-plane HA.</p>"},{"t":"Workers elect a new master automatically","ok":false,"why":"<p>Not without an HA control plane.</p>"},{"t":"Nothing happens","ok":false,"why":"<p>You lose all management and self-healing.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> etcd quorum needs an odd number of members: 3 or 5.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","level":"Senior","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s11-a-master-node-fails"},{"title":"Bring a stale branch up to date","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Your feature branch is weeks behind <code>main</code>, and you need the latest changes before opening a PR.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the cleanest approach?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Delete your branch and start again","ok":false,"why":"<p>You'd lose your work.</p>"},{"t":"<code>git fetch origin</code>, then <code>git rebase origin/main</code> (or merge it), resolve conflicts, then <code>git push --force-with-lease</code>","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. <code>--force-with-lease</code> refuses to overwrite commits you haven't seen.</p>"},{"t":"<code>git push --force</code> without fetching","ok":false,"why":"<p>That could overwrite other people's work.</p>"},{"t":"Copy files from main by hand","ok":false,"why":"<p>Error-prone, and you lose history.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> rebase your own branches; never force-push shared ones without <code>--with-lease</code>.</p>","topic":"Git","level":"Junior","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s12-bring-a-stale-branch-up-to-dat"},{"title":"Undo a pushed commit safely","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A bad commit was pushed to <code>main</code> an hour ago, and others have already pulled it.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the safe fix?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>git revert &lt;sha&gt;</code> and push. It adds a new commit that undoes the change","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. History stays intact for everyone.</p>"},{"t":"<code>git reset --hard HEAD~1</code> and force-push","ok":false,"why":"<p>That rewrites shared history and breaks teammates' clones.</p>"},{"t":"Delete the repository","ok":false,"why":"<p>Extreme and destructive.</p>"},{"t":"<code>git stash</code>","ok":false,"why":"<p>Stash only affects uncommitted local changes.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> shared branch → revert. Private branch → reset is fine.</p>","topic":"Git","level":"Junior","source":"tech-vault","links":[],"id":"s13-undo-a-pushed-commit-safely"},{"title":"Every note disappeared overnight","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The Memos ECS task was replaced overnight after a deploy. When users log back in, <strong>all their notes are gone</strong>. The Dockerfile sets <code>MEMOS_DATA=/var/opt/memos</code>, and the task definition has no volumes.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What happened, and what's the best long-term fix?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The ALB cached old responses: clear the cache","ok":false,"why":"<p>The ALB doesn't cache. Data loss after a task replacement points at the task's own storage.</p>"},{"t":"SQLite lived on the task's temporary storage, which is deleted with the task. Move the data to RDS Postgres (MEMOS_DRIVER/MEMOS_DSN) or mount EFS","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Containers are disposable, so state must live outside them. RDS Multi-AZ with the DSN from Secrets Manager is the strongest fix; EFS is the quick one.</p>"},{"t":"Increase task memory so the database fits in RAM","ok":false,"why":"<p>More memory doesn't make data survive a replacement.</p>"},{"t":"Turn on ECR scan-on-push","ok":false,"why":"<p>Image scanning has nothing to do with runtime data.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> stateless vs stateful containers. Raise this limitation yourself before the interviewer does.</p>","topic":"Reliability","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile","label":"Dockerfile"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"}],"id":"s14-every-note-disappeared-overnig"},{"title":"Is Memos highly available?","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The interviewer says: \"Your VPC spans two AZs with a NAT gateway in each. So Memos is highly available, right?\"</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the most accurate answer?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Yes: two AZs means HA","ok":false,"why":"<p>The <strong>network</strong> is multi-AZ, but there's only one task, so the <strong>application</strong> isn't.</p>"},{"t":"Not yet: <code>desired_count = 1</code> means one task in one AZ, and data is in-task SQLite. Fix the data tier, then run 2+ tasks across AZs with autoscaling","ok":true,"why":"<p>Right. A multi-AZ network is necessary but not enough. You need redundancy at every layer.</p>"},{"t":"Yes, because ECS restarts failed tasks","ok":false,"why":"<p>Restarting takes minutes and, here, loses the data. That's recovery, not HA.</p>"},{"t":"No, because ALBs only work in one AZ","ok":false,"why":"<p>ALBs actually require subnets in at least two AZs.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> HA means no single point of failure: network, compute <strong>and</strong> data.</p>","topic":"Reliability","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/vpc/main.tf"}],"id":"s15-is-memos-highly-available-"},{"title":"Pipeline says healthy, ALB says unhealthy","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>deploy.yml</code> curls <code>https://tm.myegh.com/healthz</code> and passes. But the target group marked the task unhealthy last week while the app was clearly up.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why can they disagree, and what would you change?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"They check different things: the TG requests <code>/</code> every 30 s (200–399, 2 failures = unhealthy), while CI hits <code>/healthz</code> once. Point the TG at <code>/healthz</code> and add a health-check grace period","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. <code>/</code> is the whole web UI. <code>/healthz</code> is the dedicated liveness endpoint in <code>server/server.go</code>.</p>"},{"t":"The ACM certificate is invalid for health checks","ok":false,"why":"<p>Health checks go from the ALB to the task over HTTP on 8081, not through the certificate.</p>"},{"t":"CloudWatch is delaying the results","ok":false,"why":"<p>CloudWatch doesn't run the checks. The ALB does.</p>"},{"t":"GitHub Actions uses a different DNS server","ok":false,"why":"<p>DNS isn't involved in the ALB's own health checks.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> the ALB decides health. Make the ALB and the pipeline use the same signal.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/alb/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/alb/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml","label":".github/workflows/deploy.yml"}],"id":"s16-pipeline-says-healthy-alb-says"},{"title":"503 straight after a deploy","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Right after a deploy, <code>tm.myegh.com</code> returns <strong>503 Service Temporarily Unavailable</strong>.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What should you check first?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The Route 53 TTL","ok":false,"why":"<p>DNS is fine: the request reached the ALB, which is what answered 503.</p>"},{"t":"Whether any targets are registered: ECS service events and the task's stopped reason, then logs, port 8081 everywhere and the SG chain","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. A 503 from an ALB means no registered targets, so the task isn't running or never registered.</p>"},{"t":"Renew the ACM certificate","ok":false,"why":"<p>A certificate problem fails the TLS handshake. It wouldn't produce a 503.</p>"},{"t":"Restart the NAT gateway","ok":false,"why":"<p>NAT only affects outbound traffic from the task (e.g. image pulls). Check ECS first.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> 502 = bad reply · <strong>503 = no targets</strong> · 504 = timeout. And if a release broke things, roll back first.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/security/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"}],"id":"s17-503-straight-after-a-deploy"},{"title":"Roll back a bad release fast","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A release is live and users are seeing errors. You need the previous version back <strong>now</strong>.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the fastest safe rollback with this pipeline?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>git revert</code>, then wait for the full build and deploy","ok":false,"why":"<p>It works, but it rebuilds an image and takes much longer. Use it to fix forward afterwards.</p>"},{"t":"Run <strong>Deploy</strong> manually (<code>workflow_dispatch</code>) with the previous good commit SHA. Its immutable image is still in ECR","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Immutable SHA tags guarantee you get exactly the old image, and Terraform rolls the ECS service back.</p>"},{"t":"Push the old image again as <code>latest</code>","ok":false,"why":"<p>Memos doesn't deploy <code>latest</code>, and ECR tags are immutable, so this fails.</p>"},{"t":"Delete the ECS service","ok":false,"why":"<p>That's a full outage, not a rollback.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> immutable SHA tags make rollback safe. Next step: the ECS deployment circuit breaker for <strong>automatic</strong> rollback.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml","label":".github/workflows/deploy.yml"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecr/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecr/main.tf"}],"id":"s18-roll-back-a-bad-release-fast"},{"title":"The CI role has AdministratorAccess","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A reviewer points out that <code>github-actions-role</code> has <strong>AdministratorAccess</strong>, and its trust policy allows <code>repo:EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra:*</code>.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which response is the best improvement?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Switch back to static access keys, which are easier to control","ok":false,"why":"<p>Long-lived keys are worse: they can leak and never expire.</p>"},{"t":"It's fine because OIDC is secure","ok":false,"why":"<p>OIDC removes stored keys, but whatever assumes the role still gets <strong>admin</strong>.</p>"},{"t":"Narrow <code>sub</code> to <code>ref:refs/heads/main</code> or <code>environment:production</code>, replace admin with least-privilege policies, and split a read-only plan role from the apply role","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Limit <strong>who</strong> can assume it and <strong>what</strong> they can do.</p>"},{"t":"Delete the OIDC provider","ok":false,"why":"<p>That breaks the pipeline without improving anything.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> OIDC is a strength of the project. Knowing its weak point (trust scope plus permissions) shows real understanding.</p>","topic":"Security","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/oidc.tf","label":"bootstrap/oidc.tf"}],"id":"s19-the-ci-role-has-administratora"},{"title":"The AWS bill is higher than expected","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>One small Fargate task, yet the monthly bill is much higher than you expected.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the biggest likely driver, and a sensible saving?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The Fargate task: switch to Lambda","ok":false,"why":"<p>0.25 vCPU / 512 MB is cheap. The always-on networking costs far more.</p>"},{"t":"Two NAT gateways (per hour + per GB), plus the ALB and public IPv4. Use one NAT in non-prod, an S3 gateway endpoint (free), and <code>destroy.yml</code> when idle","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. NAT gateways are the classic hidden cost. Note the trade-off: one NAT is a single point of failure.</p>"},{"t":"ECR storage of one image","ok":false,"why":"<p>Pennies.</p>"},{"t":"CloudWatch with 7-day retention","ok":false,"why":"<p>Small at this scale.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> know where cloud money goes, and say the trade-off out loud.</p>","topic":"Cost","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/vpc/main.tf"}],"id":"s20-the-aws-bill-is-higher-than-ex"},{"title":"Two applies at once","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You and a colleague run <code>terraform apply</code> on Memos at exactly the same time.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What happens?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Both succeed and the results merge","ok":false,"why":"<p>Terraform never merges concurrent applies.</p>"},{"t":"The state file gets corrupted","ok":false,"why":"<p>Locking exists to prevent exactly this.</p>"},{"t":"The second run fails with \"Error acquiring the state lock\", because <code>use_lockfile = true</code> created a <code>.tflock</code> object in S3","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. When the first apply finishes, the second person should plan again against the new state.</p>"},{"t":"The newest apply overwrites the first","ok":false,"why":"<p>Not with locking enabled.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> remote state + locking (S3 native, Terraform 1.10+). Use <code>force-unlock</code> only when you're sure nothing is running.</p>","topic":"Terraform","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/provider.tf","label":"infra/provider.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/state.tf","label":"bootstrap/state.tf"}],"id":"s21-two-applies-at-once"},{"title":"Someone opened port 22 in the console","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>During an incident, someone added an SSH rule to the ECS security group by hand in the console.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the right way to handle it?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Detect the drift with <code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code>, then remove it (or import it if it should stay). Prevent repeats: changes only via PRs, CloudTrail/Config alerts","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Code is the source of truth. Memos uses separate SG rule resources, so a hand-made rule may not even be in state.</p>"},{"t":"Leave it: Terraform will never notice","ok":false,"why":"<p>That's an unreviewed hole in your security group.</p>"},{"t":"Destroy and recreate the whole VPC","ok":false,"why":"<p>A massive overreaction that causes an outage.</p>"},{"t":"Commit the state file to Git","ok":false,"why":"<p>Never commit state.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> drift detection, plus a culture of \"no console changes\".</p>","topic":"Terraform","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/security/main.tf"}],"id":"s22-someone-opened-port-22-in-the-"},{"title":"Infra change merged, nothing deployed","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You change <code>infra/modules/alb/main.tf</code>, merge to <code>main</code>… and nothing deploys.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"tfsec silently blocked it","ok":false,"why":"<p>tfsec would fail the run loudly. Here, no run started at all.</p>"},{"t":"<code>build.yml</code> only triggers on <code>app/**</code>, <code>Dockerfile</code> and <code>.dockerignore</code>, and <code>deploy.yml</code> only runs after a build (or manually)","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Run Deploy by hand with the live SHA, or add an infra trigger plus a PR plan.</p>"},{"t":"Terraform needs a pull request to apply","ok":false,"why":"<p>Terraform doesn't know about PRs.</p>"},{"t":"The OIDC role can't modify ALBs","ok":false,"why":"<p>It's admin, so it can, and anyway the pipeline never ran.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> know your pipeline's triggers. This leads naturally to \"plan on every PR\".</p>","topic":"CI/CD","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/build.yml","label":".github/workflows/build.yml"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml","label":".github/workflows/deploy.yml"}],"id":"s23-infra-change-merged-nothing-de"},{"title":"Critical CVE in the image","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>ECR scan-on-push reports a <strong>critical</strong> vulnerability in the latest Memos image.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the best response?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Turn off scan-on-push","ok":false,"why":"<p>That hides the problem rather than fixing it.</p>"},{"t":"Find whether it's in the base image or a Go module, patch (newer <code>alpine:3.21</code> / <code>apk upgrade</code>, or bump <code>go.mod</code>), rebuild under a new SHA and deploy. Add a CI scan that fails on criticals","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Immutable tags mean the fix ships as a new version.</p>"},{"t":"Overwrite the same tag with a rebuilt image","ok":false,"why":"<p>Tags are immutable, and you'd lose traceability.</p>"},{"t":"Ignore it, because the task is in a private subnet","ok":false,"why":"<p>Private subnets reduce exposure but don't remove the risk.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> small multi-stage images and a non-root user shrink the attack surface, but you still need a patching process.</p>","topic":"Security","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile","label":"Dockerfile"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecr/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecr/main.tf"}],"id":"s24-critical-cve-in-the-image"},{"title":"Giving Memos a database password","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You've added RDS and need to give Memos its DSN, which includes a password.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Where should it go, and which role needs permission?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"In <code>terraform.tfvars</code>, committed to Git","ok":false,"why":"<p>Secrets in Git stay in the history forever.</p>"},{"t":"Baked into the Docker image as an ENV","ok":false,"why":"<p>Anyone who can pull the image can read it.</p>"},{"t":"In Secrets Manager, referenced in the task definition's <code>secrets</code>. The <strong>execution role</strong> needs <code>secretsmanager:GetSecretValue</code> on it","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. ECS fetches it at start-up, before your code runs.</p>"},{"t":"In Secrets Manager, with the <strong>task role</strong> given read access","ok":false,"why":"<p>The task role is for your app's own AWS calls. Injection at start-up uses the execution role.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> execution role (ECS: pull, logs, secrets) vs task role (your code).</p>","topic":"Security","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/iam/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/iam/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"}],"id":"s25-giving-memos-a-database-passwo"},{"title":"Tasks stop with CannotPullContainerError","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>New tasks keep stopping with <strong>CannotPullContainerError</strong>. They run in private subnets with no public IP.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which of these is <strong>not</strong> a plausible cause?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The private route table has no route to the NAT gateway","ok":false,"why":"<p>Plausible: without NAT (or VPC endpoints) the task can't reach ECR.</p>"},{"t":"The execution role is missing ECR permissions","ok":false,"why":"<p>Plausible: pulling uses the execution role.</p>"},{"t":"The ALB health check path is wrong","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct, this one is <strong>not</strong> a cause. Health checks only matter once the container is running, and pulling happens before that.</p>"},{"t":"The SHA tag doesn't exist in ECR","ok":false,"why":"<p>Plausible: deploy.yml checks this, but a manual change could skip it.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> a private task reaching ECR needs routing + SG egress 443 + IAM + the image to exist.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/vpc/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/security/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/iam/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/iam/main.tf"}],"id":"s26-tasks-stop-with-cannotpullcont"},{"title":"Re-run fails: tag already exists","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You re-run a failed pipeline on the same commit and the push fails because <code>memos:&lt;sha&gt;</code> already exists.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the right fix?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Make ECR tags mutable","ok":false,"why":"<p>That throws away the safety immutability gives you.</p>"},{"t":"Add a timestamp to every tag","ok":false,"why":"<p>Then the tag no longer maps cleanly to the commit.</p>"},{"t":"Make the build idempotent: <code>describe-images</code> first, reuse the image if it exists, build only on <code>ImageNotFoundException</code>, and fail loudly on any other error","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. That's exactly what <code>build.yml</code> does now.</p>"},{"t":"Delete the old image before each push","ok":false,"why":"<p>You'd destroy rollback targets.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> keep the safety feature and make the automation safe to re-run.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/build.yml","label":".github/workflows/build.yml"}],"id":"s27-re-run-fails-tag-already-exist"},{"title":"ECR is in the stack you destroy","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>destroy.yml</code> tears down <code>infra/</code>, which includes ECR with <code>force_delete = true</code>.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the problem, and the fix?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Destroying wipes every image, including rollback targets. Move ECR to <code>bootstrap/</code> (long-lived), drop <code>force_delete</code> and add a lifecycle policy","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Deployment artefacts have a longer lifecycle than the environment.</p>"},{"t":"No problem: images are rebuilt anyway","ok":false,"why":"<p>Rebuilding isn't guaranteed to be identical, and rollback needs the old artefacts.</p>"},{"t":"Turn off image scanning","ok":false,"why":"<p>Unrelated.</p>"},{"t":"Make ECR public","ok":false,"why":"<p>A security regression.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> group resources by <strong>lifecycle</strong>. This is the improvement from your prep doc.</p>","topic":"Terraform","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecr/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecr/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/destroy.yml","label":".github/workflows/destroy.yml"}],"id":"s28-ecr-is-in-the-stack-you-destro"},{"title":"Logs from two weeks ago are gone","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A user reports a problem from 14 days ago. CloudWatch shows nothing.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The awslogs driver was off","ok":false,"why":"<p>Recent logs exist, so the driver works.</p>"},{"t":"<code>/ecs/memos</code> has <code>retention_in_days = 7</code>, so older events were deleted. Raise retention or export to S3 for cheap long-term storage","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. It was a deliberate cost trade-off.</p>"},{"t":"CloudTrail deleted them","ok":false,"why":"<p>CloudTrail records API calls. It doesn't manage log retention.</p>"},{"t":"The NAT gateway dropped them","ok":false,"why":"<p>Logs go via the awslogs driver. Retention is the reason they're gone.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> know your retention settings, and add alarms so you hear about issues in time.</p>","topic":"Observability","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"}],"id":"s29-logs-from-two-weeks-ago-are-go"},{"title":"Why all the tfsec ignores?","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>\"tfsec runs every pipeline, but you have several <code>tfsec:ignore</code> comments. Isn't that just hiding problems?\"</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the strongest answer?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"\"tfsec has too many false positives, so I ignore it\"","ok":false,"why":"<p>Dismissive, and it sounds careless.</p>"},{"t":"\"Each ignore is a documented trade-off with a reason next to it: flow logs, Container Insights and customer KMS keys off for cost; a public ALB because it's a website; egress 443 for ECR via NAT. In a company I'd revisit them and track each as a ticket\"","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. That's judgement, not silencing a tool.</p>"},{"t":"\"I'll remove tfsec so there's no noise\"","ok":false,"why":"<p>That removes a safety net.</p>"},{"t":"\"The ignores were added by mistake\"","ok":false,"why":"<p>Not true here, and it undermines your credibility.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> interviewers want to see that you make <strong>documented</strong> trade-offs.</p>","topic":"Security","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/vpc/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/security/main.tf"}],"id":"s30-why-all-the-tfsec-ignores-"},{"title":"Container keeps dying with exit 137","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The Memos task stops every few hours with <strong>exit code 137</strong>.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's going on?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The command wasn't found","ok":false,"why":"<p>That's 127.</p>"},{"t":"SIGKILL, almost always out of memory against the 512 MB limit. Confirm in the stopped reason, raise the memory (a valid Fargate pair), find the leak and alarm on MemoryUtilization","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. 137 = 128 + 9.</p>"},{"t":"A normal shutdown","ok":false,"why":"<p>A graceful stop is 143 (SIGTERM).</p>"},{"t":"The health check port is wrong","ok":false,"why":"<p>That leads to the task being replaced, not exit 137.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> exit codes are quick wins: 0, 1, 127, 137, 143.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf"}],"id":"s31-container-keeps-dying-with-exi"},{"title":"Stand up a second region","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The business wants the platform in a new Region (e.g. me-central-1, UAE) alongside eu-west-1.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the best approach with this Terraform?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Add the new Region's resources to the existing state","ok":false,"why":"<p>One bad apply could then break both Regions.</p>"},{"t":"Reuse the modules with per-Region tfvars and <strong>separate state</strong> (its own backend key), a non-overlapping CIDR, a regional ACM cert and ECR, Route 53 routing, and a per-Region data store for residency","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. Isolate the blast radius, reuse the code.</p>"},{"t":"Copy-paste the whole repo for the new Region","ok":false,"why":"<p>Duplicated code drifts apart. Modules exist to avoid this.</p>"},{"t":"Use the same VPC CIDR in both Regions","ok":false,"why":"<p>That blocks any future peering or Transit Gateway.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> state isolation, reusable modules and data residency. That matches the role you interviewed for.</p>","topic":"Terraform","level":"Senior","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/provider.tf","label":"infra/provider.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/terraform.tfvars","label":"infra/terraform.tfvars"}],"id":"s32-stand-up-a-second-region"},{"title":"Auto-apply on main with no review","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>deploy.yml</code> runs <code>terraform apply -auto-approve tfplan</code> with no human review.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How would you add control without losing speed?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Run apply manually from your laptop instead","ok":false,"why":"<p>That loses automation and an audit trail.</p>"},{"t":"Post <code>terraform plan</code> on every PR for review, use a protected <code>production</code> environment with required reviewers for apply, and alert or block on destroys of critical resources","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. People review the plan, and the gate is enforced by the platform.</p>"},{"t":"Remove <code>-out=tfplan</code>","ok":false,"why":"<p>The saved plan is good: it guarantees you apply exactly what was planned.</p>"},{"t":"Only deploy on Fridays","ok":false,"why":"<p>That doesn't add review, and Friday deploys are a meme for a reason.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> balance speed and safety. This pairs naturally with least-privilege OIDC.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml","label":".github/workflows/deploy.yml"}],"id":"s33-auto-apply-on-main-with-no-rev"},{"title":"The .gitignore that broke the build","situation":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>CI fails with \"no Go files in …/cmd/memos\", but it builds fine on your laptop.</p>"}],"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the likely cause, based on a real Memos bug?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A <code>.gitignore</code> pattern <code>memos</code> (meant for the binary) also matched <code>cmd/memos/</code>, so <code>main.go</code> was never committed. Fix: <code>/memos</code>","ok":true,"why":"<p>Correct. A leading <code>/</code> anchors the pattern to the repo root.</p>"},{"t":"The Go version is wrong on the runner","ok":false,"why":"<p>That gives version errors, not missing files.</p>"},{"t":"CI doesn't support Go","ok":false,"why":"<p>It does.</p>"},{"t":"The Dockerfile is in the wrong folder","ok":false,"why":"<p>The file is missing from the repo itself.</p>"}],"explain":"<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> \"works on my machine\" often means files that exist locally but were never committed. Check <code>git status</code> and <code>git ls-files</code>.</p>","topic":"Memos stories","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.gitignore","label":".gitignore"}],"id":"s34-the-gitignore-that-broke-the-b"}];
